            "content": "## Why\n\nllame must answer one question on every run: *under what configuration does this run execute, and who decided each value?* Today that answer is split and inconsistent — operator defaults live in scattered environment variables, tenant-level settings have no home at all, and there is no record of what a completed run actually ran under. A self-hosted, multi-tenant assistant cannot be operated or audited that way: operators want their instance defaults version-controlled as code (the mainstream pattern for personal agents — OpenClaw, Claude Code), tenants need per-user and per-chat overrides that are isolated and concurrent-safe, and every run needs a durable, immutable snapshot so a config edit mid-flight can never retroactively change a run already in progress.\n\nThis change establishes the single, layered configuration mechanism the rest of the platform reads from — custom instructions, the tool-calling loop, the run-token budget, and the compaction threshold all become consumers of it rather than each inventing its own knob.\n\n## What Changes\n\n- **Operator config-as-code.** Introduce a version-controlled `llame.config.json` as the operator/instance configuration layer, replacing today's ad-hoc `COMPACTION_TOKEN_THRESHOLD` / `RUN_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS` / model-default environment variables as the primary source. The file is the deploy-time source of truth; it is optional (a zero-config instance still boots on documented defaults).\n- **Value interpolation for secrets and 12-factor deploys.** Config values support `{env:VAR}` (read an environment variable) and `{path:/abs/or/rel}` (read a file's contents, e.g. a Docker/Kubernetes secret) interpolation, so the file keeps JSON-config flexibility without breaking env-based deploys or forcing secrets into version control. Unresolved required interpolations fail loudly at load, never silently.\n- **Layered effective-config resolution.** A deterministic merge across scopes — **instance → org-unit → user → chat**, later wins — where objects deep-merge and arrays replace wholesale (fail-closed: a lower scope cannot append its way past a higher scope's restriction). Each resolved leaf carries **provenance** (which scope set it, at what version) and the full consulted-layer chain.\n- **Tenant configuration scopes in the datastore.** Per-org-unit, per-user, and per-chat config documents are tenant data — stored as RLS-isolated rows, not files — versioned on every write. (Config-as-code is for the *operator*; tenant overrides are runtime, concurrent, UI-editable data and belong in the database.)\n- **Immutable per-run snapshot.** At run creation, the effective config is resolved once, inside the same transaction that persists the message and run, and frozen onto the run. Execution reads only the snapshot. A config change after a run is created can never alter that run.\n- **Explain endpoint.** `GET /api/v1/chats/:id/effective-config` returns the effective config, per-leaf provenance, and consulted layers for auditability, failing closed (404, no existence leak) across tenants.\n\n## Capabilities\n\n### New Capabilities\n- `instance-config`: the operator-owned `llame.config.json` — its schema, load/validation semantics, defaults when absent, precedence over environment variables, and the `{env:…}` / `{path:…}` value-interpolation primitive (including Docker/K8s file-secret support).\n- `effective-config`: the layered resolution engine — scope precedence, object/array merge rules, per-leaf provenance and consulted-layer reporting, tenant config-scope storage with RLS isolation, the immutable per-run snapshot, and the read-only explain endpoint.\n\n### Modified Capabilities\n<!-- None at the spec level. The available-models catalog relocation into llame.config.json,\n     BYOK user-scope model sources, and the policy engine's deny-strip step are deliberately\n     downstream changes that CONSUME this one; they amend their own specs when they land. -->\n\n## 