          "input": {
            "content": "## Context\n\nllame is a self-hosted, multi-tenant assistant (VISION bet #1: multi-tenant from day one). Configuration today is fragmented: operator defaults are individual environment variables (`COMPACTION_TOKEN_THRESHOLD`, `RUN_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS`, `DEFAULT_MODEL_ID`), there is no place for per-user or per-chat settings, and nothing records what a completed run executed under. The current `stack/split-config-resolver` branch built a working layered resolver + per-run snapshot + explain endpoint, but its instance layer is env-only — which is why this redesign exists: operators want config-as-code (the mainstream personal-agent pattern), and the file-vs-database boundary must be drawn deliberately rather than accreted.\n\nThe governing distinction for this design: **operator-owned configuration is code (a file); tenant-owned configuration is data (RLS-isolated rows).** A single JSON blob cannot be the whole story for a multi-tenant system — concurrent tenant writers and cross-tenant isolation are exactly what a database provides and a shared file does not (nanoclaw is the reference contrast case: single-operator, file+SQLite, no DB-level isolation). Claude Code's layered `getSettingsWithSources()` (managed → project → user, with per-key source attribution) is the closest analogue for the operator side; the per-run *immutable snapshot* is a deliberate novelty (SPEC §6.4) that no reference comp implements.\n\n## Goals / Non-Goals\n\n**Goals:**\n- One resolution mechanism every downstream consumer reads from (custom instructions, tool loop, run-token budget, compaction threshold) — no per-feature knobs.\n- Operator config as a version-controllable file with interpolation, so secrets and 12-factor env injection both work without inlining credentials.\n- Deterministic, auditable merge: provenance per leaf, consulted-layer chain, fail-closed array semantics.\n- Durable per-run snapshot resolved atomically with message+run creation.\n- Datastore-enforced tenant isolation for the tenant config scopes.\n\n**Non-Goals (deliberately deferred to consumer changes):**\n- Relocating the hardcoded model catalog (#161) into `llame.config.json` — its own change, amends `available-models`.\n- BYOK user-scope model sources feeding the same catalog — the #137 redo.\n- The policy engine's deny-strip step (#45) and the run-budget / compaction *consumption* of the resolved values.\n- Any end-user-visible UI. This slice ships the substrate; consumers make it visible.\n- A tenant-facing write API for config documents — this change resolves and explains; how tenants *edit* their scopes is a follow-up (seeded via DB for now).\n\n## Decisions\n\n### D1. Interpolation tokens instead of file-only or env-only\n\n`{env:NAME}`, `{env:NAME:-default}`, and `{path:LOCATION}` tokens inside JSON string values, resolved at load. Chosen over (a) file-only (breaks container env-injection and forces secret files everywhere) and (b) env-only (the status quo we're leaving — no structure, no config-as-code). This is Leo's proposal and it is the right call: the file expresses *structure and defaults* under version control, while `{env:}` keeps deploys 12-factor and `{path:}` consumes Docker/K8s file-secrets. Resolution is one-pass, non-recursive (a resolved value is not re-scanned for tokens) to avoid injection surprises. Required-but-unresolved tokens are a hard startup failure — fail loud, never serve on a half-resolved config.\n\n### D2. Operator file = instance layer only; tenant scopes = DB rows\n\n`llame.config.json` populates exactly one layer (instance). Org-unit / user / chat scopes are `configs` rows keyed `(scope_type, scope_id)`, versioned per write, under RLS `ENABLE`+`FORCE`. Rationale: tenant config is concurrent, runtime-mutable, and isolation-critical — file storage would either share one mutable blob across tenants (breaks isolation) or reinvent a database on disk. This keeps the config-as-code ergonomics operators want without compromising the multi-tenant invariant.\n\n### D3. Merge: deep-merge objects, replace arrays\n\nLater scope wins; objects deep-merge; arrays replace wholesale. The array rule is a security decision, not an ergonomic one: concatenation would let a narrower (lower-trust) scope append a capability past a broader scope's restriction. Documented and tested as fail-closed.\n\n### D4. Snapshot resolved inside the message+run transaction\n\nEffective config is resolved once at run creation, in the same transaction that writes the message and run, and frozen onto the run (`config_snapshot`). Execution reads only the snapshot. Chosen over resolving live at execution time (opencode's model — a mid-flight config edit could re-steer a running turn) because durability and auditability (\"why did this run behave that way?\") outweigh always-freshest. The atomicity guarantees no run exists without its snapshot.\n\n### D5. Explain endpoint mounted under the chat resource\n\n`GET /api/v1/chats/:id/effective-config` — the chat is the resolution context in v0.3. Returns effective values + per-leaf provenance + consulted layers. Cross-tenant → 404 (no existence leak), unauth → 401. It is explicitly a *live* view (what a run created now would get), which can and should diverge from an older run's frozen snapshot.\n\n### D6. Config schema is typed and validated at load\n\nThe file is validated against a schema at startup; unknown keys and type violations fail loudly with the offending path. Keeps operator errors visible at boot rather than as silent no-ops at request time.\n\n## Risks / Trade-offs\n\n- **[Interpolation leaks a secret into logs/explain]** → Resolved secret values are never logged and never included in explain output; provenance reports *source scope*, not resolved secret content. Covered by an explicit spec scenario and a negative test.\n- **[Recursive/injection interpolation]** → One-pass, non-recursive resolution; a resolved value is treated as literal. No token re-scanning.\n- **[Array-replace surprises operators]** who expect additive lists → Documented prominently as a security-motivated, deliberate choice; the alternative is a tenant-isolation hole.\n- **[Snapshot bloat / drift from schema evolution]** — a snapshot is a point-in-time copy; later config-schema changes don't migrate old snapshots → Acceptable and intended (immutability is the point); consumers read snapshots defensively (missing key → documented default), never assume latest schema.\n- **[File precedence over env confuses migrating operators]** → One consistent rule (file wins, env is fallback), documented in `.env.example` and deployment docs, with provenance showing which source won.\n- **[Downstream consumers block on this]** → Resolver exports the accessors (max-output-tokens, compaction-threshold) now, even though nothing consumes them in this slice, so consumer changes wire against a stable seam.\n\n## Migration Plan\n\n1. Ship the tenant `configs` store (RLS ENABLE+FORCE) and `runs.config_snapshot` via migration; backfill is not required (new runs snapshot forward; absent snapshot → resolve-from-defaults read path for legacy runs, or treat as empty).\n2. Introduce `llame.config.json` loader + interpolation as the instance layer; keep the legacy instance env vars working as documented fallbacks/interpolation targets so existing deploys don't break on upgrade.\n3. Update `.env.example` and deployment docs: env vars are now fallbacks; the file is the primary operator surface.\n4. Amend SPEC §6.3–§6.4 and add a VISION \"emerging directions\" note on the operator/tenant config boundary.\n5. Rollback: the file is optional and env fallbacks remain, so reverting to env-only is a config change, not a code rollback; the `configs` table and snapshot column are additive.\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- **Config hot-reload vs. restart-to-apply** for `llame.config.json` — restart-only is simpler and sufficient for v0.3 (operator config changes are deploy events); revisit if operators need live reload. *Leaning: restart-only, documented.*\n- **Tenant config write API** — out of scope here; needs its own change (validation, which scopes a given role may write, version-conflict handling). Seeded via DB in the interim.\n- **Schema surface** — how much of the config shape to formalize now (just `run.maxOutputTokens` + `compaction.tokenThreshold`, the two live keys) vs. reserving namespaces for tools/policy/models. *Leaning: define only the live keys; reserve top-level namespaces by convention, not schema, to avoid churn.*\n",
