            "content": "## Context\n\nllame's operator/system configuration is today a scatter of environment variables read ad hoc via `ConfigService` (`app.module.ts` loads `.env.local`; `DEFAULT_MODEL_ID`, `TITLE_GENERATION_MODEL_ID`, `COMPACTION_TOKEN_THRESHOLD`, `RUN_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS`, `MODEL_CONTEXT_WINDOW_TOKENS`). There is no single version-controllable operator surface and no way to combine JSON structure with env/secret injection.\n\nThis change is the first slice of a larger rethink of configuration. That rethink concluded (via a grill/explore session) that there are **two distinct configuration concerns**, and conflating them into one generic layered \"config document\" resolver was the wrong model:\n\n```\nCAPABILITY COMPOSITION (\"what can I use\")     SETTINGS (\"defaults / thresholds\")\n  models, tools, connectors — SETS             per-model threshold, default model, …\n  system ∪ union(memberships) ∪ mine           each has its OWN natural resolution:\n  then policy deny-overrides-allow subtracts     threshold = model-default → user-per-model → per-send\n  → available-models + policy subsystems         → typed, not a generic deep-merge\n```\n\nNeither concern is served by a generic `configs(scope_type, scope_id, jsonb)` table with flat deep-merge — models compose by union (not merge), and thresholds are model-keyed (their default lives on the model definition, not in a config scope). So the generic resolver explored on `stack/split-config-resolver` (#131) is **not merged**; it is superseded. What survives from that exploration as future work: a per-run *settings snapshot* (freeze what a run resolved, for audit) applied to typed settings — a separate downstream change.\n\nThis change carves out the one piece that is unambiguously correct and unblocking: **operator/system config as code** — a file with a strict schema and interpolation. It is the operator side of the operator-code / tenant-data boundary; tenant (user/chat) settings stay in the DB under RLS and are out of scope here.\n\n## Goals / Non-Goals\n\n**Goals:**\n- One version-controllable file for operator/system settings, replacing scattered env vars as the primary path.\n- Keep JSON structure while supporting env injection and file-mounted secrets, via `{env:…}` / `{path:…}` interpolation.\n- Fail loud on any misconfiguration at boot (config-as-code = deploy-time correctness).\n- A stable mechanism that later changes (model-catalog relocation, per-model defaults) extend rather than reinvent.\n\n**Non-Goals (owned by other changes):**\n- Tenant (user/chat) settings storage — DB + RLS, a separate change.\n- The per-run settings snapshot and any explain/provenance surface.\n- Relocating the #161 model catalog into the file (this change ships the mechanism + migrates the loose scalars; the catalog object moves in its own change).\n- Any generic cross-scope layered \"config document\" resolver — explicitly dropped.\n- Hot-reload / live file-watching.\n\n## Decisions\n\n### D1. Interpolation tokens (`{env:}`, `{env:-default}`, `{path:}`), single-pass\n\nChosen over file-only (breaks container env injection; forces secret files everywhere) and env-only (the status quo we're leaving). The file expresses structure and defaults under version control; `{env:}` keeps deploys 12-factor; `{path:}` consumes Docker/K8s file-secrets. Single-pass, non-recursive resolution (a resolved value is literal, never re-scanned) avoids injection surprises. Required-but-unresolved tokens are a hard boot failure.\n\n### D2. Strict, closed schema (unknown key → fail)\n\nChosen over permissive pass-through. For security-relevant config (future `tools`, `policy` namespaces), a silent typo is a governance hole. Strict validation makes each config key deliberately registered; the coupling cost (every consumer change extends the schema) is *good* coupling. Validation runs at boot so operator errors surface at deploy, not as silent no-ops at request time.\n\n### D3. File-over-env precedence, env as fallback\n\nOne consistent rule: file wins, env is fallback, built-in default last. Lets an operator migrate a setting from env to file with no behavior change and no flag day. The current instance env vars remain honored as fallbacks/interpolation targets so existing deploys don't break on upgrade.\n\n### D4. Restart-to-apply, not hot-reload\n\nOperator config is a deploy-time concern. Hot-reload adds file-watching, atomic-swap, and partial-write races on a security-relevant file for the sole benefit of \"new runs pick up new config without restart\" — which a restart handles anyway. Documented as restart-only. (A bad file failing the boot is the intended fail-loud behavior.)\n\n### D5. Mechanism first, contents migrated minimally\n\nThis change ships the loader/validator/interpolation and migrates the *currently loose* instance scalars (model default id, title-gen model id, instance-level defaults) into the file. It does **not** relocate the full model-catalog object or introduce per-model threshold keying — those ride with the catalog change that consumes this mechanism. Keeps the slice small and reviewable.\n\n## Risks / Trade-offs\n\n- **[Interpolation leaks a secret into logs]** → Resolved `{env:}`/`{path:}` values are never logged; errors name the source (variable/path), never the resolved value. Explicit spec requirement + negative test.\n- **[Recursive/injection interpolation]** → Single-pass, non-recursive; resolved values are literals.\n- **[Strict schema couples every consumer change to the schema module]** → Accepted deliberately (D2): the alternative is silent typo no-ops on security-relevant keys. Merge-point cost is the price of catching typos at boot.\n- **[Bad file fails the boot]** → Intended. A config typo is a failed deploy, not a silent fallback to defaults. Documented so operators expect deploy-time validation.\n- **[Operators confused by file-vs-env precedence]** → One documented rule (file > env > default) with `.env.example` and deploy-doc updates.\n\n## Migration Plan\n\n1. Add the loader + interpolation + strict schema; wire it ahead of the current `ConfigService` reads for the migrated keys, with env as fallback.\n2. Ship a documented `llame.config.json` example; update `.env.example` and deploy docs to frame the env vars as fallbacks/interpolation targets.\n3. Amend SPEC (config section) to describe the operator config-as-code layer; add a VISION \"emerging directions\" note on the operator-code / tenant-data boundary and the two-concern (capability vs settings) split.\n4. Rollback: the file is optional and env fallbacks remain, so reverting to env-only is a config change, not a code rollback.\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- **Config file location & override** — default path (repo/app root) and an env override for it (e.g. `LLAME_CONFIG_PATH`). *Leaning: documented default path + single override var.*\n- **How much schema to define now** — only the currently-migrated scalars, or also reserve `models` / `tools` / `policy` namespaces. *Leaning: define only migrated keys; reserve namespaces in docs, add to the schema when their consumer change lands (keeps strict-closed honest without pre-committing shapes).*\n",
