            "content": "// llame operator configuration (config-as-code).\n//\n// Optional — llame boots on documented built-in defaults when this file is\n// absent (but chat needs defaults.modelId, so a real instance wants this\n// file). Copy this example to `llame.config.json` (same directory by default,\n// or wherever LLAME_CONFIG_PATH points):\n//   cp apps/api/llame.config.json.example apps/api/llame.config.json\n//\n// Format: JSONC — // and /* */ comments and trailing commas are allowed.\n// Validation: validated at boot against the published JSON Schema\n// (src/instance-config/llame.config.schema.json, referenced below via\n// $schema) — a strict, closed schema. Unknown keys and wrong types fail the\n// boot loudly, naming the offending path; llame never serves on a partial or\n// silently-defaulted config.\n// There is no hot-reload — changes take effect on restart.\n//\n// Precedence per setting: file > built-in default. Bare environment variables\n// are NOT a config source — the environment reaches these settings only via\n// {env:NAME} interpolation tokens written in this file (as the entries below\n// demonstrate). A key that is absent (or set to explicit `null`, for nullable\n// settings) is simply unset and takes the built-in default.\n//\n// String values may interpolate:\n//   {env:NAME}            the environment variable NAME (fails boot if unset)\n//   {env:NAME:-default}   NAME, or \"default\" if NAME is unset OR empty\n//                         (bash/compose `:-` style)\n//   {path:LOCATION}       trimmed contents of the file at LOCATION (Docker/K8s\n//                         file-mounted secrets), e.g. {path:/run/secrets/openai_key}\n//   {{                    a literal \"{\" (double it to escape) — e.g. the string\n//                         \"prefix-{{\" resolves to \"prefix-{\"\n// Interpolation is single-pass: a resolved value is a literal and is never\n// re-scanned for further tokens. Resolved values are never logged.\n//\n// None of the settings in this first slice are secrets, so none of them need\n// {path:...} here — it exists for a future setting like `providers[].key`,\n// e.g. \"key\": \"{path:/run/secrets/openai_key}\".\n//\n// Embedded (mid-string) tokens are only legal on string-typed settings, like\n// \"defaults.modelId\" below. Number/boolean settings must use a WHOLE-VALUE,\n// quoted token that gets coerced to the right type after resolution — never a\n// bare number with an embedded token, and never an unquoted token. See the\n// \"runs\" block below.\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"./src/instance-config/llame.config.schema.json\",\n\n  // Instance-level model pointers — deliberately not under a \"models\" key,\n  // which is reserved for the future model-catalog list (providers-and-models-\n  // as-code follow-up, #167). The {env:...:-default} tokens keep these\n  // drivable from the environment (see apps/api/.env.example) while the file\n  // stays the single source of truth.\n  \"defaults\": {\n    \"modelId\": \"{env:DEFAULT_MODEL_ID:-system:openai:gpt-5.4-mini}\",\n    \"titleGenerationModelId\": \"{env:TITLE_GENERATION_MODEL_ID:-system:openai:gpt-5.4-nano}\"\n  },\n\n  // Run liveness (#48). heartbeatSeconds/heartbeatStaleSeconds/timeoutSeconds\n  // are numbers, so an interpolated value on them must be a whole-value quoted\n  // token — the resolved string is coerced to a number after resolution; a\n  // non-numeric resolution fails the boot.\n  \"runs\": {\n    // Reserved for the future run-output-budget feature (#91) — the schema\n    // accepts this key today, but nothing consumes it yet: setting it here\n    // does NOT currently cap a run's output tokens.\n    \"maxOutputTokens\": null,\n    // How often the executing worker stamps the run's heartbeat.\n    \"heartbeatSeconds\": \"{env:RUN_HEARTBEAT_SECONDS:-15}\",\n    // How stale a heartbeat may get before the deadman job expires the run.\n    \"heartbeatStaleSeconds\": \"{env:RUN_HEARTBEAT_STALE_SECONDS:-60}\",\n    // Deadman timeout after enqueue.\n    \"timeoutSeconds\": \"{env:RUN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS:-300}\"\n  },\n\n  \"http\": {\n    // Reverse-proxy trust (#68). Nullable:\n    //   - `null` (or key absent)   -> not behind a proxy; proxy headers untrusted\n    //   - \"{env:TRUST_PROXY:-}\"    -> drivable from the TRUST_PROXY env var;\n    //                                 resolves to empty = unset when the var\n    //                                 is unset or blank\n    //   - a hop count (e.g. \"1\") or an Express subnet spec -> literal setting\n    \"trustProxy\": \"{env:TRUST_PROXY:-}\"\n  }\n}\n",
