            "content": "## Context\n\n`#44` shipped the org-unit/membership data model, RLS, and HTTP surface (`identity.controller.ts`), plus a first web admin UI at `apps/web/app/(chat)/settings/organizations/` (`org-tree`, `members-panel`, `org-unit-dialogs`, `role-picker`, `api-error-message`). That UI is a settings sub-page with flat indentation. The [Admin.dc.html design](https://claude.ai/design/p/9b8618ce-8b93-4e02-81dd-4c5e9e166841?file=Admin.dc.html) moves administration to a dedicated area and makes the tree real.\n\nThis proposal was handed off as a UI/IA-only brief (`proposals/org-admin-restructure.md`). It was **analyzed, not accepted blind** — three corrections drive the design below.\n\n## Corrections to the source brief (verified against the codebase)\n\n1. **Not UI-only.** The design surfaces a member count and the caller's role on every node — and the brief's own \"membership at a glance\" scenario mandates it — but `OrgUnitResponse` returns only `{id, parentId, name, type, path, settings, createdAt}`. Rendering it needs `memberCount` + `directRole` on the list response. This is a read enrichment over the existing `memberships` table: **no datastore/schema change**, one added `COUNT`/`GROUP BY` and the caller's own membership join. (Inherited role is derived client-side from the path — no server change for that.)\n2. **Gating is a seam, not wired.** The brief gates the admin area on \"SPEC #158 (`root_org_creation`)\". That mechanism **does not exist** (no spec, no code). The admin area is the same org data behind the same RLS/403 guards it already has, so it is not a new privileged surface. Create-root gating is specified as a hook that binds *when* #158 lands; until then, honest server-403 handling — never a fabricated client-only admin check.\n3. **Projects stay; no unification.** `project` is already an `org_unit_type` (owner direction: a project is \"just a chat group\" — the unit where chats live). All five types coexist in the tree. The brief's \"projects-as-org-units follow-up\" is dropped (already true). The genuine follow-up is chat↔project association — out of scope here.\n\n## Goals / Non-Goals\n\n**Goals:**\n\n- A dedicated `/admin` area with its own shell/section-nav, org-tree as the only built section.\n- A real tree matching the design: guides, chevrons, type icons, counts, role badges, hover actions, selected-unit footer, and the create/child/rename/move/delete dialogs.\n- Pre-emptive leaf-first delete and move-picker-excludes-subtree UX (mirroring server invariants, not reimplementing authz).\n- The minimal read enrichment (`memberCount` + `directRole`) that the per-node design requires.\n- Rehome the existing members panel; keep its spec and the domain-error-copy spec unchanged.\n\n**Non-Goals:**\n\n- Building the Users/Providers/Connectors/Policies/Audit sections (placeholders only).\n- The `#158` instance-admin gating mechanism itself.\n- chat↔project association (the actual \"projects contain chats\" feature).\n- Any datastore/RLS/schema change; any new org endpoint.\n\n## Decisions\n\n### D1. `/admin` route group with its own layout, entered from the user menu\n\nA new Next.js route group (`app/(admin)/` or `app/admin/`) with its own layout renders the design's admin shell (left section-nav + main). The chat app-shell/user-menu gains an \"Administration\" entry. `/settings/organizations` becomes a redirect to `/admin/organizations` (deep links preserved). The current settings sub-page's components are moved, not rewritten from scratch, where they still fit.\n\n### D2. Tree affordances are presentation over the existing list data + the two new fields\n\nThe tree (guides/chevrons/type icons/counts/role badges/hover actions) is built from the unit list plus `memberCount`/`directRole`. Expand/collapse and collapse-all are client state. Inherited-role display walks the client-side path (nearest ancestor with a `directRole`), exactly as the design's `effectiveRole` does — no server call per node.\n\n### D3. Read enrichment: `memberCount` + `directRole` on the unit list\n\n`OrgUnitResponse` gains `memberCount: number` and `directRole: OrgRole | null`. The list query adds a `COUNT` of membership rows per unit and a left-join to the caller's own membership row. Visibility is unchanged — the same units the caller can already see, gated by the same path-role RLS the roster requirement uses. Belongs to `org-memberships` (it is membership-derived read data) even though it rides the org-unit list response.\n\n### D4. Pre-emptive invariants, not client authz\n\nTwo UX pre-checks mirror server invariants that are *structural facts the client already knows*, not authorization: \"has children\" (→ leaf-first delete explanation, delete disabled on non-leaf) and \"is in my subtree\" (→ excluded from move targets). Neither is an authz decision (those stay server-side, 403 handled honestly). This is the line the brief draws and it is correct.\n\n### D5. Projects render as ordinary units; project-as-leaf is the open question\n\nProjects appear in the tree with the folder type icon, member count, and role badge like any unit. The one unresolved product-semantics question (below) is whether a `project` node offers \"add child unit\" or is treated as a leaf (chats-only container). The schema permits nesting either way; this is a UI affordance decision, not a data constraint.\n\n### D6. Design-system conformance\n\nConnectors are neutral-ink hairlines (`color-mix(in oklab, var(--foreground) 20%, transparent)` per the design), type icons and selection use tokens, no new hue, no colored active bar — passes DESIGN.md §10.\n\n## Risks / Trade-offs\n\n- **[Enrichment N+1 if done naively]** → do it as one aggregate query (`COUNT ... GROUP BY unit` + caller-membership left join), not per-unit round trips. Verified the roster RLS already scopes visibility, so no extra auth surface.\n- **[Gating seam misread as \"gated now\"]** → spec explicitly says the signal doesn't exist yet and forbids a fabricated client check; the create-root affordance relies on server 403 until #158.\n- **[Relocation breaks deep links]** → `/settings/organizations` redirect preserves them; covered by a scenario.\n- **[Members panel drift]** → it is ported, not rebuilt; its spec is untouched, so behavior is unchanged.\n- **[Project nesting confusion]** → resolved by the open question; until decided, mirror the design (projects can hold children) rather than invent a constraint.\n\n## Migration Plan\n\n1. API: add `memberCount` + `directRole` to `OrgUnitResponse` and the list query; regenerate `openapi.json`; unit + RLS tests for the visibility of the summary.\n2. Web: `/admin` route group + layout + section nav; move org-tree/members-panel/dialogs/role-picker in; rebuild the tree to the design; `/settings/organizations` redirect; Administration entry in the shell/user menu.\n3. Pre-emptive delete/move UX; empty state; selected-unit footer with the existing members panel.\n4. Design-system review pass (§10).\n5. Docs: CHANGELOG; note the dropped/renamed follow-ups (chat↔project association; #158 gating).\n\n## Open Questions (for the grill)\n\n1. **Project as leaf?** Does a `project` node offer \"add child unit\", or is it a chats-only container with no sub-units (owner framing: \"a project is just a chat group\")? *Leaning: project-as-leaf — no add-child on project nodes — which matches the chat-group meaning; revisit if a use case for nesting under a project appears.*\n2. **Route + label:** `/admin` + \"Administration\" (matches the design). *Leaning: yes.*\n3. **`directRole` field placement:** on `OrgUnitResponse` (rides the unit list, one call) vs a separate `/units/effective-roles` batch. *Leaning: on the unit list — the design renders them together, and one call is simpler than a second round trip.*\n",
