            "content": "## Why\n\nllame's product surface (README, SPEC §1) treats **projects** as first-class shared workspaces — \"their own chats, knowledge, connectors, skills, artifacts, and members.\" None of that exists yet: a chat belongs to exactly one user (`chats.owner_user_id`) with no way to group chats or share them. The org-unit tree (#44) is governance (org → team → department), not a place a person keeps and shares a body of work.\n\nThis change lays the **projects foundation**: a project is, at bottom, a **shareable chat group** — a workspace that owns chats and has invited members. It is modeled after the GitHub repo: **user-owned or org-owned**, with **invited collaborators** on top of ownership. This first slice ships the user-owned half with real sharing; org-ownership and org-membership inheritance are designed-in but wired later.\n\n## What Changes\n\n- **New `projects` entity** — a terminal workspace (no child units; it holds members and chats, not sub-projects), stored in its **own `projects` table** (not an `org_unit_type`). Polymorphic owner via two mutually-exclusive nullable FKs: `owner_user_id` (text → `users`) **or** `owner_org_unit_id` (uuid → `org_units`), with an exactly-one-set CHECK. **This slice wires user ownership only**; `owner_org_unit_id` ships as a column + FK + CHECK arm but is always null for now.\n- **Invited membership** — a new `project_memberships` table (`project_id`, `user_id`, `role`) with a lean **`project_role` enum: `owner` / `editor` / `viewer`** (deliberately *not* the seven-value `org_role`). Direct members are invitable on the project from day one. Org-membership **inheritance** (an org-owned project granting access via its owning unit's roster, nearest-wins) is a **future arm** — the resolution seam is designed here, not built.\n- **Chats join a project** — `chats.project_id` (nullable uuid FK → `projects`, `ON DELETE SET NULL`): a chat belongs to **0 or 1** project; deleting a project unfiles its chats, i