            "content": "## ADDED Requirements\n\n### Requirement: Project entity and ownership\n\nThe system SHALL provide a **project** as a first-class, terminal workspace that owns chats and has members. A project SHALL NOT contain child projects or any nested unit — it holds members and chats only. A project SHALL have **exactly one owner**, which is EITHER a single user OR a single org unit, never both and never neither. Projects SHALL be stored as their own entity, distinct from org units.\n\nIn this first slice the system SHALL wire **user ownership** only; org-unit ownership SHALL be representable (an owning-org-unit reference exists and the exactly-one-owner rule admits it) but SHALL NOT be creatable through the project surface yet.\n\n#### Scenario: A user creates a project they own\n\n- **WHEN** a signed-in user creates a project\n- **THEN** the project is persisted with that user as its sole owner, and it appears in that user's project list\n\n#### Scenario: Exactly-one-owner is enforced by the datastore\n\n- **WHEN** a project row would be written with neither owner set, or with both a user owner and an org-unit owner set\n- **THEN** the datastore rejects the write (the exactly-one-owner rule is a stored constraint, not only application logic)\n\n#### Scenario: Projects are terminal\n\n- **WHEN** any attempt is made to nest a project under another project or give a project a child unit\n- **THEN** there is no surface to do so — the project model exposes members and chats only\n\n### Requirement: Invited project membership and roles\n\nA project SHALL have **members** drawn from users, each holding exactly one **project role** from the vocabulary `owner`, `editor`, `viewer`. A project's owner SHALL be able to **invite** another user as a member with a chosen role, **change** a member's role, and **remove** a member. `viewer` SHALL be read-only; `editor` SHALL be able to add and remove chats in the project; `owner` SHALL additionally be able to manage members and delete the project. Role checks SHALL be enforced in the datastore, not only in application code, and SHALL fail closed when the caller's identity is absent.\n\nOrg-unit-owned projects granting membership by **inheriting** their owning unit's roster is out of scope for this slice; the effective-membership resolution SHALL be defined so that inheritance can be added later without changing the direct-membership model.\n\n#### Scenario: Owner invites a member\n\n- **WHEN** a project owner invites another user as an `editor`\n- **THEN** that user gains `editor` access to the project and sees it in their project list\n\n#### Scenario: Only an owner manages membership\n\n- **WHEN** a non-owner member attempts to invite, remove, or change the role of a member\n- **THEN** the action is denied\n\n#### Scenario: Viewer cannot modify project content\n\n- **WHEN** a `viewer` member attempts to add or remove a chat in the project\n- **THEN** the action is denied\n\n### Requirement: Chat association with a project\n\nA chat SHALL belong to **at most one** project. The chat's owner (and any member permitted to modify project content) SHALL be able to **file** a chat into a project and **unfile** it. Deleting a project SHALL **unfile** its chats — the chats and their conversation history SHALL survive; a deleted project SHALL NOT cascade-delete chats.\n\n#### Scenario: Filing a chat into a project\n\n- **WHEN** a chat owner files their chat into a project they can modify\n- **THEN** the chat is associated with that project and appears grouped under it\n\n#### Scenario: Deleting a project preserves its chats\n\n- **WHEN** a project that contains chats is deleted\n- **THEN** the chats are unfiled (no longer associated with any project) and remain readable by their owner; no conversation history is lost\n\n### Requirement: Project members can read the project's chats\n\nA user who is a member of a project (owner, editor, or viewer) SHALL be able to **read** the chats filed into that project and their messages, even when they are not the chat's individual owner. This visibility SHALL be enforced in the datastore (defense-in-depth), SHALL be scoped to exactly the chats filed into projects the caller is a member of, and SHALL fail closed when identity is absent.\n\n#### Scenario: A member reads a shared project's chat\n\n- **WHEN** a project member who does not individually own a chat opens a chat that is filed into that project\n- **THEN** they can read the chat and its messages\n\n#### Scenario: A non-member is denied a project's chats\n\n- **WHEN** a user who is not a member of a project attempts to read a chat filed into that project (and does not individually own it)\n- **THEN** access is denied — the chat and its messages are not returned\n\n#### Scenario: Membership visibility is scoped to the member's own projects\n\n- **WHEN** a user is a member of project A but not project B\n- **THEN** they can read chats filed into A but are denied chats filed into B\n\n### Requirement: Unfiled and unshared chats remain owner-only\n\nIntroducing project sharing SHALL NOT widen access to chats that are not filed into a project. A chat with no project association SHALL remain readable only by its individual owner (and via existing public-share behavior), exactly as before this change.\n\n#### Scenario: An unfiled chat is unaffected by project membership\n\n- **WHEN** a user is a member of some project, and another user owns an unfiled chat\n- **THEN** the member cannot read that unfiled chat — project membership grants no access outside the projects they belong to\n\n#### Scenario: Existing owner and public-share behavior is preserved\n\n- **WHEN** a chat is private and unfiled\n- **THEN** only its owner can read it; when it is public, existing public-read behavior is unchanged\n\n### Requirement: Project management HTTP surface\n\nThe API SHALL expose project lifecycle and membership operations as REST resources: create/read/list/update/delete a project, list/add/update-role/remove a member, and file/unfile a chat. Each endpoint SHALL take a validated request DTO and return an explicit response type (code-first OpenAPI), SHALL derive the acting identity only from the authenticated session (never from client-supplied ownership/scope input), and SHALL surface authorization denials honestly rather than silently returning empty results.\n\n#### Scenario: Listing projects returns only the caller's projects\n\n- **WHEN** a user lists projects\n- **THEN** the response contains exactly the projects they own or are a member of, and no others\n\n#### Scenario: Identity is server-derived\n\n- **WHEN** a request carries a client-supplied owner or member id that differs from the authenticated user\n- **THEN** the server ignores the client-supplied identity and authorizes against the authenticated session\n",
