            "content": "## Context\n\nllame has no way to group or share chats. A chat has a single `owner_user_id` (text → `users`; NextAuth-convention text id) with `FORCE` RLS (`chats_owner`, `chats_public_read`) and messages inherit that via `messages_owner`. The org-unit tree (#44) is governance, not a personal/shared workspace, and its membership model is path-inheritance over `org_units` + `memberships` with a `BYPASSRLS` helper (`llame_role_on_unit_path`) that exists specifically to break a two-table RLS recursion (`org_units.select` scans `memberships`; a `memberships` policy scanning `org_units` back would cycle — documented at length in apps/api/CLAUDE.md).\n\nThis change adds **projects**: a terminal, shareable chat group with a polymorphic owner (user or org unit) and invited members. The user-owned half ships with real sharing; org-ownership + inheritance are designed-in, wired later.\n\nOwner direction (verbatim intent): *\"Other users may be invited to both other users' projects and org-owned projects, and in future, when we add org-owned projects, they should inherit memberships of their org unit.\"* So the direct-membership model must be complete now, and the effective-membership resolution must leave room for an inheritance arm without reshaping the tables.\n\n## Goals / Non-Goals\n\n**Goals:**\n\n- A `projects` entity in its **own table** (not an `org_unit_type`): terminal, polymorphic owner, exactly-one-owner enforced by the datastore.\n- Invited membership with a lean `owner`/`editor`/`viewer` role set.\n- `chat.project_id` (0-or-1); filing/unfiling; project delete unfiles (never destroys) chats.\n- **Datastore-enforced sharing**: project members can read the project's chats + messages; non-members cannot; unfiled chats stay owner-only. Negative tests are the acceptance criteria.\n- The polymorphic owner and the effective-membership seam are shaped so org-ownership + org-roster inheritance compose in later with no table reshape.\n\n**Non-Goals:**\n\n- Wiring `owner_org_unit_id` (org-owned projects) or org-roster **inheritance** — schema-ready, not built.\n- **Member-authored content in a shared chat** — posting messages / running the agent *as a collaborator* in someone else's chat raises run-identity/BYOK/attribution questions out of scope here. This slice shares **read** access + role-gated project/chat-filing management; authoring in a shared chat is a follow-up.\n- A project's knowledge / connectors / skills / artifacts (SPEC §1) — later slices.\n- The `memberships → org_unit_memberships` rename (separate change; see below).\n\n## Decisions\n\n### D1. Projects are their own table, not `org_unit_type = 'project'`\n\nA project is terminal (no path, no children), has a **different owner model** (a user *or* a unit — org units are always unit-parented), and a **different, smaller role vocabulary**. Forcing it through `org_units` would drag in the materialized-path machinery it never uses and the seven-value `org_role` it doesn't want, and would make user-ownership a special case in a table built for unit nesting. Separate table, separate membership table.\n\n- *Alternative rejected:* reuse `org_units` with `type='project'` and `memberships`. This is what the current `org_unit_type` enum's `project` value anticipated; it couples a flat, user-ownable workspace to path-inheritance and the wrong role set. The `project` enum value becomes dead and is dropped in a follow-up.\n\n### D2. Polymorphic owner = two mutually-exclusive nullable FKs + a CHECK\n\n```\nowner_user_id      text  REFERENCES users(id)      ON DELETE CASCADE   -- nullable\nowner_org_unit_id  uuid  REFERENCES org_units(id)  ON DELETE CASCADE   -- nullable\nCHECK ( (owner_user_id IS NOT NULL) <> (owner_org_unit_id IS NOT NULL) )  -- exactly one\n```\n\nReal FKs (typed, indexed, referential-integrity-checked, RLS-friendly) beat a `owner_type`/`owner_id` stringly pair with no FK. `owner_user_id` is `text` to match `users.id`; `owner_org_unit_id` is `uuid` to match `org_units.id`. This slice only ever sets `owner_user_id`; the org arm is present so the constraint and the future path exist from day one.\n\n- *Alternative rejected:* single `owner_type`/`owner_id` polymorphic pair — loses FKs and forces string-typed joins in RLS; the two-column form is the standard Postgres polymorphism that keeps integrity.\n\n### D3. `project_role` enum: `owner` / `editor` / `viewer`\n\nA project is a folder-of-chats, not a governance unit; three roles cover it (delete/manage · modify content · read). A dedicated enum keeps project semantics from drifting onto `org_role` and vice-versa.\n\n- *Alternative rejected:* reuse `org_role` (7 values) — over-broad; `maintainer`/`guest`/`service_account` have no project meaning yet, and coupling the enums means one can't evolve without the other.\n\n### D4. `project_memberships` — direct membership only, with an inheritance-ready resolver seam\n\n```\nproject_memberships(\n  id uuid pk, project_id uuid FK projects ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  user_id text FK users ON DELETE CASCADE, role project_role,\n  created_at, UNIQUE(project_id, user_id) )\n```\n\nEffective role is resolved by a single function `resolveProjectRole(project, user)`:\n\n- **now:** the caller's direct `project_memberships` row, plus an implicit `owner` when `project.owner_user_id = user`.\n- **future (org-owned):** union the above with the nearest-wins role from the owning unit's roster (`llame_role_on_unit_path(project.owner_org_unit_id, …)`), org-owner treated as `owner`.\n\nOnly the \"now\" arm is implemented; the resolver is the single place the inheritance arm slots into, so no policy or table changes when it lands.\n\n### D5. RLS — reuse the org-units recursion-break pattern, but keep the `BYPASSRLS` footprint minimal\n\nBidirectional project↔membership visibility is the **same `42P17` recursion class** as org-units: `projects.select` must scan `project_memberships` (a member sees their project), so any `project_memberships` policy that scans `projects` back closes the cycle. The proven break is a `SECURITY DEFINER` / `BYPASSRLS` helper, provisioned exactly like `llame_role_on_unit_path` (migration `CREATE FUNCTION` + grant; `docker/postgres/rls-function-owner.sql` reassigns ownership to `app_rls`; `pnpm db:provision-rls`).\n\nCrucially, the helper is needed for **only two** policies — everything else stays on row-local / own-membership-row scans that don't recurse:\n\n| Surface | Policy | Uses helper? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `projects` SELECT | `owner_user_id = current_user OR id IN (SELECT project_id FROM project_memberships WHERE user_id = current_user)` | No — own-rows scan; `project_memberships.select` own-row branch is row-local |\n| `projects` UPDATE/DELETE | `owner_user_id = current_user` | No — ownership is a column on the row |\n| `project_memberships` SELECT | `user_id = current_user OR llame_project_role(project_id, ARRAY['owner','editor'])` | **Yes** — co-member roster visibility is the cycle |\n| `project_memberships` INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE | `llame_project_role(project_id, ARRAY['owner'])` | **Yes** — only the owner manages membership |\n| `chats` SELECT (additive) | `chats_project_member`: `project_id IS NOT NULL AND project_id IN (SELECT project_id FROM project_memberships WHERE user_id = current_user)` | No — own-membership-row scan |\n| `messages` SELECT (additive) | `messages_project_member`: chat_id in the caller's project-filed chats (restate the chats condition, like `messages_owner`) | No |\n| `chats` UPDATE (file/unfile withCheck) | target `project_id` is one the caller owns (`projects.owner_user_id`) or is an `editor` of (own membership row) | No |\n\n`chats_project_member` / `messages_project_member` are **additive permissive SELECT** policies — they OR alongside the untouched `chats_owner` / `chats_public_read` / `messages_owner` / `messages_public_read`, so they only widen reads to project members and never weaken the owner/public paths. Both tables keep `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`.\n\n- *Alternative rejected — generalize `llame_role_on_unit_path` to cover projects:* projects are a separate table with a separate membership table and role enum; overloading the unit-path function couples them. A sibling `llame_project_role(project_id, roles[])` is the right altitude — same pattern, own domain.\n- *Alternative rejected — no helper; own-rows-only `project_memberships.select` + service-context roster read after app authz:* avoids a second `BYPASSRLS` function but reintroduces exactly the \"elevated read after app-layer authz = weaker defense-in-depth\" fallback apps/api/CLAUDE.md flags as a last resort. For a tenant-boundary change, pay for the datastore-enforced version. (Grill: confirm we want the helper vs. accept the weaker roster read.)\n\n### D6. HTTP surface — RESTful, DTO + explicit response type, server-derived identity\n\n`projects` NestJS module (controller/service/repository) under `api/v1/projects`: project CRUD, `projects/:id/members` (list/add/patch-role/remove), and chat filing as a `PATCH /chats/:id` field (`projectId`) — not an RPC verb, per the resource-design convention. Acting identity comes only from the session; `openapi.json` regenerated. All project reads run inside `TenantDbService.runAs` so RLS is engaged.\n\n## Risks / Trade-offs\n\n- **[First cross-user read path on the most sensitive tables]** → enforce in RLS (`FORCE`), fail closed on absent identity, and ship negative tests: non-member denied a project's chats *and* messages; a member of A denied B's chats; unfiled chats stay owner-only. `scripts/rls-test.sh` (unique `RLS_TEST_PORT`) must stay green.\n- **[Second `BYPASSRLS` function extends provisioning]** → `llame_project_role` follows the documented `llame_role_on_unit_path` split (migration creates + grants; `rls-function-owner.sql` reassigns owner to `app_rls`); `pnpm db:reset && db:migrate && db:provision-rls` sequence unchanged. Footprint held to two policies.\n- **[Additive `chats`/`messages` policies could accidentally OR-widen owner reads]** → they are `SELECT`-only and gate on project membership; a test pins that an owner query still returns only owner+member-visible rows and that `relforcerowsecurity` is still true on both tables.\n- **[`resolveProjectRole` divergence between now and future arms]** → single resolver function is the one seam; the inheritance arm is a union added there, and its absence is asserted by a test that org-owned projects can't be created yet.\n- **[Deleting a user who owns projects]** → `owner_user_id … ON DELETE CASCADE` deletes their projects (which unfile, not delete, chats via `chat.project_id ON DELETE SET NULL`); confirm this matches the chats' own `owner_user_id … ON DELETE CASCADE` semantics so a user delete is consistent.\n\n## Migration Plan\n\n1. Schema (drizzle-kit, migration `0022`): `project_role` enum; `projects`; `project_memberships`; `chats.project_id` (+ index). Hand-append `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` for `projects`/`project_memberships` and `CREATE FUNCTION llame_project_role` + grants (like `0019`); add the additive `chats`/`messages` member policies. Update `rls-function-owner.sql` to also reassign `llame_project_role` to `app_rls`.\n2. API: `projects` module + DTOs + response types; chat-filing on `PATCH /chats`; regenerate `openapi.json`.\n3. Web: personal Projects surface (list/create/open) + sidebar grouping of chats by project; thin API client.\n4. Verify: `pnpm --filter api build/test/typecheck/lint`; `bash apps/api/scripts/rls-test.sh` (unique port) incl. the new negative cases; `db:provision-rls` in the fresh-DB sequence.\n5. Docs: CHANGELOG; note follow-ups (org-owned + inheritance; member-authored shared content; drop `project` from `org_unit_type`).\n\n## Open Questions (for the grill)\n\n1. **Chat membership of exactly one project (0-or-1) vs many** — leaning **0-or-1** (`chat.project_id` nullable FK; a folder, not a tag). Confirm.\n2. **Who may file/unfile and invite** — leaning **ow