er it's actually a problem.\n6. **Drizzle conventions mostly hold.** `CREATE EXTENSION` and `FORCE RLS` already have the `--custom`-migration precedent (migration 0004's FORCE note); tsvector is a `customType` + `.generatedAlwaysAs()`, GIN via `.using`. No wholesale \"raw SQL migrations\" carve-out needed.\n7. **Doc gaps to decide, not blockers:** compaction summaries as searchable documents (they're high-signal — I'd say yes, but v2); forked chats duplicating content across lineage (accept duplicate hits in v1); `matchedBy` result contract is good, keep it.\n\n## Your 4-job split — endorsed, with amendments\n\n**Job 1 (FTS + trigram, no embeddings)** is bigger than it sounds and that's correct: it builds the *platform* — `search_documents` projection, deterministic chunker (parts-aware, versioned, content-hashed), pg-boss reindex job, RLS policies, and the new query (FTS + `word_similarity`, rank-fused at two legs). Do the projection here, not direct-on-messages indexing — otherwise job 2 rebuilds job 1. Two riders: fix **#171** here (the web palette must stop re-filtering server results through cmdk anyway once results carry ranks/snippets), and **build the relevance eval set here** — you can't judge job 3's embedding lift without a job-1 baseline. Closes/refs #143, #171.\n\n**Job 2 (embedding layer, no search)** = model registry + `search_embeddings` + one `EmbeddingBackend` + backfill + batch pipeline. Zero query-path risk while the async machinery bakes in production. Blocked on two decisions:\n- *Infra:* swap to a pgvector-enabled image (`pgvector/pgvector:pg17`) in compose, `rls-test.sh`, CI — and document pgvector as a self-host deployment requirement (README/AGENTS).\n- *Product (BYOK tension):* whose credentials embed the chats? Background indexing is instance-scoped, not per-request — I'd put the embedding model in `llame.config.json` (operator concern, like model defaults; matches the config-two-concerns architecture), with a local/Ollama backend (bge-m3-class multilingual) as the flagship self-hosted answer and no-config = no embeddings, search stays lexical. This \"works with zero external providers\" is itself differentiation.\n\n**Job 3 (hybrid search + RRF)** = query embedding (synchronous, active model) + three-leg fusion + weighted top-3 chat aggregation + eval-driven tuning. Thin once 1–2 exist. Ship behind measurement: if the eval shows lexical+trigram already clears your bar for ru/en/es, you can even delay activation.\n\n**Job 4 (episodic memory)** — the doc doesn't cover this at all; it's a search doc. Be precise about what job 4 adds over job 3, because the tool *already* rides the search path: (a) temporal semantics — time-range filters, \"when did we discuss X\", date/chat provenance in tool output; (b) recency as *ranking signal only*, never deletion (gbrain lesson from your memory research); (c) recall-time injection framing — recalled snippets marked as data, not instructions (Hermes lesson: sanitize at recall, `[recalled memory, not new input]`); (d) maybe auto-recall injection into new runs — defer, tool-first. Crucially, your July memory research verdict (**verbatim > extraction**) says the search projection *is* the episodic store — no fact-extraction pipeline, which is exactly why this plan is cheap and why most competitors (extraction-based memory) will be worse at it.\n\nOne honest check on the differentiation claim: jobs 1–3 are *quality*, not uniqueness — Open WebUI et al. just do it badly. The differentiator is job 4 sitting on 1–3: a self-hosted assistant whose recall over its own verbatim history is hybrid, multilingual, provider-optional, and RLS-isolated. That combination is genuinely uncommon.\n\n## Readiness + plan (by leverage)\n\n1. **Start job 1 now** — no blockers, no new infra, closes two open bugs, and produces the eval baseline everything downstream needs. Next step: openspec proposal for it.\n2. **Decide the two job-2 gates** (pgvector image swap; operator-config embedding 